  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The pressure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> of diatomic gas <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>&#947;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math> changes suddenly to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mrow><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively during an adiabatic process. The temperature of the gas increases and becomes____times of its initial temperature.&nbsp;</p><p>(given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></math>)</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "4",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>For adiabatic process,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>&#947;</mi></msup><mo>=</mo></math> constant</p><p>We know, density&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>mass</mi><mi>Volume</mi></mfrac></math>.</p><p>Then, we have&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>&#947;</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo></math> constant</p><p>Given here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#947;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>.</p><p>The ratio&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><mi>&#947;</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>32</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>128</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>The ratio of temperature using ideal gas equation is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>128</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Thus,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}